hink that  we saw certainly the  nurses being made into commodities it's  like we need nurses we talk about beds  and you know how many beds in a hospital  but beds need nurses to work those  beds or else they're not really valuable  and we've gone from this thing of of  nurses for hire bringing in nurses from  other states we can pay them more our  nurses then leaving to work someplace  else to be paid more  i mean the recognition of  the the feeling value the feeling the  love from your home institution has felt  sort of lacking and there are strategies  that people can take to prevent burnout  that hospitals can use when nurses feel  valued when they have voice when they  have shared governance all those things  make people stay  on the job more so let's talk about  something that is somewhat political  something that was discussed a little  while ago in an interim committee uh  with lawmaker state lawmakers this idea  of a nursing licensure compact sounds a  little technical here but essentially  it's it's a group of states all grouping  together  to help share resources to some extent  can you explain what this  is nevada's not a part of it right now  that is not a part and we've tried  periodically and it looks like we may be  trying again the nursing compact  licensure compact is a way of making  i would say standardizing or upping the  game of nursing practice across the  country when you think about it nursing  schools are  educating students with the kind of a  core curriculum that our creditors  require  and we all take the same licensure test  to get our license but with each state  having their own rules agreements it  means that nurses work differently  across states and what we're really  looking for is a way to have nurses work  at the top of their license full scope  of license that really benefits patients  and so 37 states joining in this have  said we agree that we're going to have  this standard and that  all the states that are part of us agree  to this standard work with this standard  why aren't more states involved 37 means  there's a fair chunk of them that are  not part of this  can you at least touch on why there  might be some hesitation in the nursing  community to being a part of this  i think nurses as a whole  like the idea of of compacts some of the  concerns people have are  making are more about disciplinary  things so people are concerned about if  they're nurses who are problems in one  state then suddenly you've opened the  door for them to bring those problems to  our state they're um people often  verbalize that maybe we'll lose control  over what happens within our state  um  i would say 37 is well over half and so  and it's been a journey  probably since 2010 or something that  we've really been moving at how can we  make  nursing practice the same across the  country  and so um  most of the fears that people have the  data doesn't bear out and data doesn't  show that this is really a legitimate  concern  our efforts to speak with local nursing  unions and state lawmakers on this were  unsuccessful utah and arizona are part  of the nursing interstate compact  next on politics now stopping violent  crime the new federal operation to try  and crack down on crime spikes in major  cities  vegas good luck  you're watching politics now  the byte administration is facing  mounting pressure to address a spike in  violent crime in major cities alexander  limon explores the steps the  administration is taking to address that  and what officials would like to see  the justice department says it has been  working to combat violent crime and that  a 30-day operation carried out by the  u.s marshals service resulted in a large  number of arrests including of three  suspects in connection with the mass  shooting on philadelphia's south street  the justice department says in june  federal law enforcement arrested 1500  violent criminals through something  called operation north star which is an  example of our efforts to protect our  communities from violence  and gun violence attorney general  merrick garland says the operation  focused on criminals wanted for the most  serious offenses including homicide in  10 major u.s cities this initiative  reflects  the  anti-violent crime initiative that we've  had in place since the beginning of  2021. however officials say the country  continues to deal with crime issues  notably with ongoing mass shootings most  recently a gunman opened fire from a  rooftop on a crowd celebrating  independence day in highland park  illinois  so far authorities say seven people have  died 20 minutes from the house i grew up  in it's just another horrific reminder  of the violence and particularly the gun  violence that we face authorities say  that gunman has now confessed to the  crime and he had ar-15 style weapons  president biden and vice president  harris are renewing their calls to ban  assault weapons and that's a call that  lake county state's attorney eric  rinehart echoes  a ban of these types of weapons between  1994 and 2004 everything shows that  these types of horrifying devastating  incidents went down during that time  but right now there appears to be no  appetite among republican lawmakers to  ban assault weapons last month congress  did pass new gun laws that include  expanding background checks and  encouraging states to pass red flag laws  as well as dedicating more funding to  mental health  in washington alexandra limon  republican senate candidate adam  laxalt's annual bask fry is adding more  guests former acting u.s attorney  general under president trump matthew  whitaker is scheduled to attend senator  texas cruz from texas is also speaking  at the event this is held up in  gardnerville every august  next on 8 news now remembering 1 october  how you can weigh in on what happens at  the site of the worst mass shooting in  american history this is politics  weekdays on cbs  you're watching politics now  clark county leaders are now accepting  ideas for a permanent one october  memorial this is of course the the  healing garden  you can submit those ideas on the clark  county website the deadline is september  30th later this year a design team will  be chosen and a formal recommendation on  those plans could happen in late summer  next year clark county is also hosting  informational sessions about this  process later this week and next week as  well  this week in what to watch rent control  is on the agenda the city of north las  vegas has a special city council meeting  next tuesday to verify the signature and  signature on a culinary union-backed  rent control initiative it is being  dubbed neighborhood stability by the  union we will let you know how that goes  forward  that'll do it for us here on politics  now we thank you for joining us here of  course we're back with you next saturday  for all the latest in the political news  just head to 8newsnow.com  have a good rest of your weekend we'll  be back with you next saturday take care  [Music]
 

